er, excellent computer skills, weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues. Candidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. Candidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as know-it-all, hot temper.\n\nWe should respond to the current discussion: Agents 1 and 3 both recommended B, noting communication concerns. We could bring up additional concerns about B (weak memory for numbers, nasty remarks) that affect crew cooperation and reliability. Or we could argue that Candidate A's anticipation and leadership outweigh B's communication issues. Or bring up Candidate C or D.\n\nProbably we should challenge B by adding more concerns: B makes nasty remarks about colleagues, which is serious for crew cooperation. Also weak memory for numbers could affect attention and accuracy. So we might shift recommendation to Candidate A or C. Let's evaluate: Candidate A has leadership, anticipation, spatial vision, but also show-off, not open to new ideas, unfriendly. Those are also crew cooperation concerns. Candidate C has quick decision making, calm in crisis, understands tech, but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education. Candidate D has responsibility, problem solving, concentration, but hot temper, know-it-all, loner.\n\nWhich is best? B has strong reliability and stress, but communication issues severe (nasty remarks). A has leadership but also unfriendly. C has decision quality and calmness, but egocentric and communication difficulty. D has responsibility but hot temper.\n\nProbably B still strongest despite issues? But we can argue that B's nasty remarks are a red flag for crew cooperation, more severe than A's show-off. So maybe we still recommend B but note need for training. Or we could